        'In how many years will Le 2,500 amount to Le 3,400 at 6% simple interest per annum?',
        'Interest $I = 3400 - 2500 = $ Le 900.

$T = \dfrac{100I}{PR} = \dfrac{100 \times 900}{2500 \times 6} = \dfrac{90\,000}{15\,000} = 6$ years

**Why the other options are wrong**

- 4.4 years: You used the amount (Le 3,400) as the principal. The principal is the money first invested: Le 2,500.
- 16.7 years: You turned the formula upside down. $T = \dfrac{100I}{PR}$.
- 22.7 years: You used the amount as the interest. Interest = amount − principal = Le 900.',
        3)
