        'A tape measure gives the length of a table as 1.5 m. The actual length is 1.6 m. Find the percentage error.',
        'Error $= 1.6 - 1.5 = 0.1$ m.

Percentage error $= \dfrac{\text{error}}{\text{actual value}} \times 100\% = \dfrac{0.1}{1.6} \times 100\% = 6.25\%$

**Why the other options are wrong**

- 0.1%: 0.1 m is the error in metres. Divide it by the actual length, then multiply by 100.
- 6.67%: You divided by the measured length. Percentage error uses the actual length.
- 93.75%: That is the measured length as a percentage of the actual length.',
        5)
